window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][107] = "<h1>Part XI — Career Track (personalized)</h1>\n<span id=\"c37\"></span>\n<h2>C37 — The 12-Month Plan, Readiness Checklist and Your First 90 Days</h2>\n<span id=\"c37-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;How have you prepared for this interview, and what did you learn about yourself?&quot; &quot;How do you keep up with this industry?&quot; &quot;How do you decide what to learn when there is too much?&quot; &quot;What would you do in your first 90 days?&quot; Interviewers ask about preparation and ramp-up because they predict how you will learn on the job: whether you diagnose before you act, practice what you are weak at, use evidence, and turn learning into work that others can see. This chapter converts the whole book into a dated plan for one reader: someone who graduated in October 2024, has about two years in a product role, and has twelve months. It gives a baseline, a month-by-month plan with a reading map, a weekly cadence, projects that become interview stories, a literacy habit built on verified sources, a practice toolkit, a readiness gate, a fallback and, if you get the offer, a first-90-days plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code>: behavioral and motivation questions about learning and growth, and the hiring-manager conversation about how you would start <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. It is also the working plan for everything else in this book.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether your preparation and your ramp-up plan are specific, evidence-led and honest <code>Inferred</code>: a measured baseline, a plan driven by gaps, a feedback loop, a way to know you are ready, and a realistic first 90 days that ends in something shipped and written down.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You start from a measured baseline, not a feeling.</li>\n<li>You allocate time by importance and gap, and re-measure on a schedule.</li>\n<li>You practice by producing answers, and get outside scoring.</li>\n<li>You turn work into stories with numbers that are true.</li>\n<li>You decide when to apply by a written gate, not by anxiety or a calendar.</li>\n<li>You plan the first 90 days around learning, relationships and one small shipped result.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end preparation answers: &quot;I've been reading a lot,&quot; a plan with no checkpoints, practicing the favorite round, applying to the top choice first, no fallback, and a first-90-days plan that is &quot;meet everyone and learn the product.&quot;</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Dates to check before you rely on this book.</strong> Several facts here carry dates that will pass during a twelve-month plan. They were checked in October 2026. Check each one yourself at the time you read this book, not only at its date, and treat anything marked as of 2026-10 the same way. Read each company's current posting yourself; do not rely on this table.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What</th>\n<th>Date in the book</th>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th>What to check</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Google's 2027-start Associate Product Manager application window</td>\n<td>Closed at the end of 6 October 2026, per Google's posting</td>\n<td>C01.4, C33</td>\n<td>Whether the window moved, and when the next cycle opens; dates varied within the 2025 cycle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meta's Rotational Product Manager program</td>\n<td>Open per Meta's posting of 23 September 2026 (March and September 2027 starts); the posting lists 4+ years of related experience as a minimum; no closing date found</td>\n<td>C01.4, C33, C37</td>\n<td>The posting's eligibility line and closing date; whether eligibility changes again</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>UPI merchant fee in India</td>\n<td>15 October 2026</td>\n<td>C09</td>\n<td>Whether the fee started as announced; accounts differ on who announced it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NPCI's 30% cap on any one UPI app's share of volume</td>\n<td>31 December 2026, after two extensions</td>\n<td>Appendix A.8</td>\n<td>Whether it takes effect or is extended again</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>India's data protection rules</td>\n<td>13 November 2026 (consent managers) and 13 May 2027 (main obligations)</td>\n<td>C09, C21, C25</td>\n<td>The phase dates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EU AI Act</td>\n<td>2 December 2026 (marking by generative systems already on the market), 2 December 2027 and 2 August 2028 (high-risk systems)</td>\n<td>C25</td>\n<td>The dates set by the 2026 amending regulation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>US state AI law</td>\n<td>1 January 2027, Colorado's narrower law on automated decision-making technology</td>\n<td>C25</td>\n<td>Its status; no comprehensive federal AI statute was in force through July 2026</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Program rules, loop formats and rules on AI tools in interviews</td>\n<td>Any time</td>\n<td>C01, C33</td>\n<td>Each company's careers page and interview guidance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prices, model names and product features</td>\n<td>Any time</td>\n<td>C20, C24, C25, C28</td>\n<td>Anything marked as of 2026-10</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c37-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c37-c37-1-diagnostic-baseline-against-the-bands\"></span>\n<h4>C37.1 Diagnostic baseline against the bands</h4>\n<p>You cannot plan from a feeling, and C34 showed why: fluency is not evidence (CS-C34-02). The baseline gives you numbers (FW-C37-01).</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1. Three recorded mocks, outside-scored.</strong> In your first two weeks, record three mock rounds of different types (for example product design, a metrics question and a behavioral story), and have an independent scorer rate each on the C34 scorecard (1 to 5, where 2 is Baseline, 4 is Strong and 5 is Standout) without hearing your own scores first. If you have no partner yet, score the recordings yourself a week later against the written anchors and treat the numbers as provisional; get an outside scorer as soon as you can.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2. Map to the ten competencies.</strong> Average the scores by competency (C01.6): product sense, discovery, problem solving, analytics and experimentation, execution, technical fluency, AI product thinking, design and UX, strategy and go-to-market, behavioral and communication. Three mocks will not cover all ten; mark untested ones &quot;unmeasured&quot; and schedule a mock for each before the first checkpoint.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3. Compute importance times gap.</strong> Use the importance weights in C01.6 (3 for high, 2 for medium), the gap as 5 minus your score, and allocate drill hours in proportion (FW-C01-04; Worked example 1).</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4. Record a calibration estimate.</strong> After six mocks with an outside scorer, compute your bias (mean self-score minus outside score) and your error (mean absolute difference) (FW-C34-03). A positive bias means you overrate yourself; subtract it from your self-scores until it shrinks.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 5. Set numeric targets and dates.</strong> For the two largest importance-times-gap dimensions, set a target (for example &quot;analytics from 2.9 to 3.8 by the second checkpoint&quot;) and a re-score date.</p>\n<p><strong>What the baseline is not.</strong> It is not a verdict on whether you will get an offer; one outside scorer on three mocks is noisy, and the scores are the author's synthesis of observable behaviors, not any company's rubric <code>Original</code> <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<span id=\"c37-c37-2-the-twelve-month-plan-month-by-month\"></span>\n<h4>C37.2 The twelve-month plan, month by month</h4>\n<p><strong>Assumptions (adjust them to your life).</strong> About nine hours a week for 48 weeks (52 weeks minus four of rest or travel), so about 432 hours; a start in the first week of October 2026 and an end in the last week of September 2027; access to at least two practice partners from month 2; and the profile in C01 (about two years in a product role). If you have six hours a week, use the fast track in the reading map below; with twelve, add depth in your weakest area.</p>\n<p><strong>The hours budget (FW-C37-02).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Activity</th>\n<th>Hours a week</th>\n<th>Hours in the year</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Reading and worked examples</td>\n<td>2.0</td>\n<td>96</td>\n<td>First read of each chapter on your path, and recomputing its worked examples by hand</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timed drills</td>\n<td>2.5</td>\n<td>120</td>\n<td>Exercises and questions answered aloud on the clock, allocated by importance times gap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Partner mocks and scoring</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>72</td>\n<td>One scored mock a week with an outside scorer from month 2 (before that, recorded solo drills)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Projects that become stories</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>72</td>\n<td>Work that produces true, numbered stories (C37.4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Literacy habit</td>\n<td>1.0</td>\n<td>48</td>\n<td>The weekly routine in C37.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Journal and review</td>\n<td>0.5</td>\n<td>24</td>\n<td>Error log, answer journal and the monthly re-score (C34.5)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total</strong></td>\n<td><strong>9.0</strong></td>\n<td><strong>432</strong></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why it fits.</strong> The other 34 chapters of the book are about 637,000 words, which is about 53 hours of first reading at 200 words a minute (a planning estimate). Reading and recomputing the worked examples roughly doubles that, to about 106 hours, a little above the 96-hour reading budget; the difference is why the deep dives and the reference library are chosen by need and not all read. Drills, mocks and projects are the rest of the year, and they are where most of the learning is. The word counts are those of the chapters when the book was written, and the hours follow from the rates stated here.</p>\n<span id=\"c37-the-reading-map-reading-map\"></span>\n<h5>The reading map (<code>reading-map</code>)</h5>\n<p>A <strong>reading map</strong> is a ranking of the chapters of this book into a core path, deep dives and a reference library, with the hours each costs, so that the book's length does not burden a reader on a twelve-month plan. Study hours are twice the first-read hours (read, then recompute the worked examples); the figures are planning estimates (invented rates, 200 words a minute). The reading map is the author's judgment of importance by interview weight (C01.6) <code>Original</code> <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Track</th>\n<th>Chapters</th>\n<th>First read (h)</th>\n<th>Study (h)</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Core path (21 chapters)</strong></td>\n<td>C01, C02, C03, C05, C06, C07, C08, C12, C13, C14, C15, C16, C17, C18, C21, C24, C25, C31, C32, C33, C34</td>\n<td>34.6</td>\n<td>69.3</td>\n<td>Every reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deep dives (8 chapters)</strong></td>\n<td>C04 estimation, C09 new markets, C10 UX and quality, C11 critique method, C19 platforms, C20 go-to-market, C22 system design, C23 machine learning and ranking</td>\n<td>12.2</td>\n<td>24.4</td>\n<td>By gap and by target company</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reference library (5 chapters)</strong></td>\n<td>C26 zero to one, C27 domain primers, C28 teardown library, C29 anti-patterns, C30 five journeys</td>\n<td>6.3</td>\n<td>12.5</td>\n<td>Lookup, practice material and templates</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The fast track (about 80 hours in all).</strong> For a reader with fewer weeks or hours: C01, C02, C03, C05, C07, C12, C13, C15, C16, C31, C32, C33 and C34 (13 chapters; about 21 hours of first reading and 43 of study), plus about 23 hours of timed drills, about 12 hours of partner mocks and about 4 hours of stories and journal. Add C24 for AI roles. The fast track covers the high-weight competencies; it leaves out depth.</p>\n<p><strong>Chapter-by-chapter hours by month</strong> (study hours; deep dives can be swapped by gap).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Month</th>\n<th>Dates</th>\n<th>Chapters</th>\n<th>Study (h)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Oct 2026</td>\n<td>C01, C02, C34 (sections 1 to 4)</td>\n<td>6.4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Nov 2026</td>\n<td>C03, C05, C31 (the story bank and signal sections)</td>\n<td>8.2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Dec 2026</td>\n<td>C06, C07, C32</td>\n<td>10.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Jan 2027</td>\n<td>C12, C13, C04</td>\n<td>10.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Feb 2027</td>\n<td>C14, C08, C11</td>\n<td>10.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Mar 2027</td>\n<td>C15, C16, C17</td>\n<td>9.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Apr 2027</td>\n<td>C18, C21, C22</td>\n<td>9.9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>May 2027</td>\n<td>C24, C25, C23</td>\n<td>9.8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Jun 2027</td>\n<td>C31 (the rest), C33, C09</td>\n<td>9.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Jul 2027</td>\n<td>C34 (the rest), C19, C20, C10</td>\n<td>10.2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Aug 2027</td>\n<td>The reference library, by need (C26 to C30)</td>\n<td>12.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Sep 2027</td>\n<td>No new chapters; review and the final-week protocol</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>Total</strong></td>\n<td><strong>106.2</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c37-the-plan-month-by-month-fw-c37-02\"></span>\n<h5>The plan, month by month (FW-C37-02)</h5>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Month</th>\n<th>Focus</th>\n<th>Deliverables</th>\n<th>Checkpoint or gate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1 (Oct 2026)</td>\n<td>Baseline and setup</td>\n<td>Three outside-scored mocks; the baseline table; fit-checked postings (C01.5); the toolkit (C37.6); the literacy routine started; a first story-bank list of 10 episodes</td>\n<td>Decide this week which, if any, October windows to use (see &quot;Application waves&quot;)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2 (Nov 2026)</td>\n<td>Foundations: structure and metrics</td>\n<td>Two partners recruited; weekly mocks begin; sandbox set up and first funnel query; first project chosen</td>\n<td><strong>Checkpoint 1:</strong> re-score, calibration estimate after 6 mocks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3 (Dec 2026)</td>\n<td>Discovery and product design</td>\n<td>Project 1 (metrics audit); story bank drafted with numbers; first product-design drills</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4 (Jan 2027)</td>\n<td>Analytics and diagnosis</td>\n<td>Statistics and metric-diagnosis drills; Project 2 (a small analysis or experiment)</td>\n<td><strong>Checkpoint 2</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5 (Feb 2027)</td>\n<td>Experiments, improvement, critique</td>\n<td>Project 2 readout; three teardowns with sources (C28 as models)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6 (Mar 2027)</td>\n<td>Execution and partnership</td>\n<td>Project 3 (a spec with non-goals, reviewed by an engineer); first company briefs for three targets (C33)</td>\n<td><strong>Checkpoint 3</strong>; first practice applications (wave 0 to 1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7 (Apr 2027)</td>\n<td>Strategy and technical fluency</td>\n<td>System-design and strategy drills; platform and ecosystem examples</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8 (May 2027)</td>\n<td>AI product management</td>\n<td>Evaluation-design and autonomy-ladder drills; an AI-feature spec with an evaluation plan</td>\n<td><strong>Checkpoint 4</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9 (Jun 2027)</td>\n<td>Behavioral, communication and loops</td>\n<td>Stories polished and probed; Project 6 (an influence story); company briefs final</td>\n<td>First full mock loop</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10 (Jul 2027)</td>\n<td>Mock loops and the gate</td>\n<td>Three full mock loops with outside scoring; targeted drills</td>\n<td><strong>Readiness gate test 1</strong> (FW-C37-06)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11 (Aug 2027)</td>\n<td>Gap closure and applications</td>\n<td>Drills on the remaining gaps; apply to top targets as the gate passes</td>\n<td><strong>Checkpoint 5</strong>; applications (wave 2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12 (Sep 2027)</td>\n<td>Peak readiness and fallback</td>\n<td>The final-week protocol (C34.10) before each loop; the fallback plan decided (FW-C37-08)</td>\n<td>Gate re-test before each loop</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Application waves.</strong> <code>Inferred</code>: many early-career programs recruit on an annual cycle with a fall window, and direct associate roles are posted through the year <code>[V: Google's and Meta's own pages: annual application windows]</code>, so the dates of your top-choice applications matter. Read the company's current posting yourself at the time you read this book. Use three waves.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Wave 0 (months 1 to 3):</em> a few fit-checked roles where a miss costs little, to learn the process and get real feedback. Do not use your top-choice company here.</li>\n<li><em>Wave 1 (months 6 to 8):</em> mid-priority targets once checkpoint 3 shows the high-weight dimensions near Strong.</li>\n<li><em>Wave 2 (months 9 to 12):</em> top targets, only after the readiness gate passes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>An October 2026 decision.</strong> As of this writing, Google's 2027-start window has closed (6 October 2026) and Meta's program, though open, lists a four-year experience minimum, so this decision applies to other programs' windows and, for those two, to the next cycle. If you are eligible for a program whose window closes in October 2026 and it is your top target, decide in the first week using your baseline: apply if your outside-scored average on the high-weight dimensions is at least about 3.5 and you can complete a last-week protocol before a loop; otherwise plan for the next cycle and use this window's postings as practice for roles where you are eligible. This is an exception to the waves below: it spends a once-a-year window on your top target before the readiness gate has been passed, so accept it only knowingly. Ask the recruiter how reapplication works, because policies differ and I could not verify them <code>[unverified: reapplication policies not found on the pages read]</code>. Do not understate your experience or dates to fit a window (C01.5). Read the company's current posting yourself at the time you read this book.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked how they prepared, a candidate says: &quot;I read two books and did a lot of practice questions.&quot; The interviewer asks what the candidate's weakest area was and how they knew. The candidate has an activity list and no baseline, no measure of weakness and no evidence of change. A better answer: &quot;My first three outside-scored mocks averaged 2.9 on analytics against 3.8 on product sense, so I put the largest share of drill hours there; after four months and 20 scored mocks, my analytics average was 3.9 and my self-scores matched the outside scorer within half a point.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>When not to follow the plan as written.</strong> If your hours, dates or targets differ, change the plan but keep its structure: baseline, gap-driven hours, outside scoring, checkpoints, a gate. Do not delay applications indefinitely: a gate is a decision rule, not a reason to wait for perfection, and one loop is one draw from a selective process (C01.7).</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Getting to the interview: the minimum.</strong> The plan above prepares you to pass a loop. A loop happens only after a recruiter or hiring manager decides to talk to you, and four parts of that step do not date: the resume bullet, the referral request, the recruiter screen and the posting. What does date (which programs open when, who is eligible, where roles sit) is in C01.4, C01.5 and the Dates box earlier in this chapter; check those yourself at the time you read this book. This box gives the minimum. A full treatment of resumes, applications and offers is left to the Second Edition. The advice is <code>Inferred</code> from common recruiting practice and from candidate reports, not from any company's rubric.</p>\n<p><strong>1. The resume bullet.</strong> A bullet that gets read names a decision you made or influenced, the evidence behind it, and a measured change. Use this shape: a verb for what you decided, what it was, the evidence, and what moved from what to what. Three before-and-after pairs follow. All are <code>Illustrative</code>: the work and the numbers are invented.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Before: &quot;Responsible for improving onboarding for the mobile app.&quot; After: &quot;Cut onboarding from seven steps to four after funnel data showed 38% of new users (invented) quitting at step five; completion rose from 52% to 61% (invented) in six weeks.&quot;</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Before: &quot;Worked with engineers to launch a feature.&quot; After: &quot;Chose to release saved search to 10% of users (invented) first, set a stop rule of a two-point drop in checkout conversion, and released to everyone after four weeks with no drop.&quot;</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Before: &quot;Analyzed user data.&quot; After: &quot;Found that 60% of support tickets (invented) came from two billing screens, wrote the one-page case, and got both redesigned; tickets fell 31% (invented) in a quarter.&quot;</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Check each bullet before you keep it. It states a decision, not a duty. It has one number with a base, so the reader knows what the number moved from. It uses a verb that shows your role (chose, wrote, ran), not &quot;helped&quot;. It fits on one or two lines. Above all, you can answer three follow-up questions about it, because an interviewer will ask them; C31 shows how stories are probed.</p>\n<p>If you have no honest number, use a count you can defend, such as users interviewed or tickets read, and never a percentage you cannot explain. A quick test: if you cannot say what changed because of you, the line describes a duty and needs rewriting. A bullet only has to make the reader ask about the decision; the full account belongs in the story, not on the page. Precise numbers raise credibility and raise questions, so keep only those you could rebuild from memory in a minute. Put your six to eight strongest bullets where a reader looks first <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>2. The referral request.</strong> A referral asks one person to put your name in front of another, so make the request easy to grant and easy to decline. Send one ask per message, link the exact posting, attach a one-page resume, and give a two-sentence reason you fit. Follow up once after a week, then stop. Thank the person whether or not it leads anywhere <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<p>With a contact you already have, such as a former colleague or classmate (<code>Illustrative</code>: the names and numbers are invented): &quot;Hi Priya, I am applying for the Associate Product Manager posting on your team's page (link below). Last year I ran a checkout test that raised completion from 61% to 64%. Would you refer me, or point me to the right person? My one-page resume is attached. No problem if it is not possible, and thank you either way.&quot;</p>\n<p>Without a network, do not write to a recruiter to ask for a referral. Write to a person in a relevant role with one question that has a short answer, shows you read their work, and costs them two minutes (<code>Illustrative</code>: the names are invented): &quot;Hello Daniel, I read your post on ranking changes. I am preparing for product roles and have one question: how did your team choose which metric to protect when two moved in opposite directions? If you have ten minutes next month, I would value your view.&quot; Ask for a referral only after a real exchange.</p>\n<p>If the answer is yes, send a short note the person can forward: two lines on who you are and why the role fits, plus the resume. Still apply through the official posting yourself, because a referral usually adds to an application and does not replace it <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<p>Limits. A referral does not replace fit, and some employers route referrals through a portal with its own rules, which you should read. Do not send the same message to many people at one company.</p>\n<p><strong>3. The recruiter screen.</strong> Candidate reports describe an early call, often short, that checks role fit, motivation, communication and logistics before any product question <code>Inferred</code>. Prepare five things.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><em>A sixty-second introduction:</em> your current role, one decision with its number, and why this role next. Say it aloud and time it. An <code>Illustrative</code> version (invented): &quot;I am a product analyst at a retail app. Last year I found that most support tickets came from two screens, got them redesigned, and tickets fell by a third. I want a team that owns a product end to end, and this role does.&quot;</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><em>Why this company and this role:</em> one product you have used or studied and what you would want to work on, not general praise.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><em>Logistics, stated plainly:</em> where you can work, when you could start, and your work authorization. Ask for the range for the level before you give a number <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><em>Three questions of your own:</em> the format and length of the loop, who you would meet, and what the team works on.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><em>One line for each gap:</em> a short tenure, a career change or a break in dates needs one plain sentence you can say without apology, followed by the next point.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common failures are rambling past two minutes, reading from notes, telling a full story that belongs in the loop, and not knowing which role you applied for. If you do not know an answer, say you will confirm it and follow up. Take notes, write down the names you hear, and send a two-line thank-you the same day. The screen is not a product interview, so keep answers short and save the depth for the rounds.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Reading a posting.</strong> A posting is evidence of what the team will screen for. Read it in four passes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><em>Requirements:</em> copy each one into a list and mark it yes, partly or no, with the experience that backs your mark. Treat a stated minimum, such as years of experience, as a filter that is usually applied unless the posting says otherwise <code>Inferred</code>. C01.5 and its fit check (FW-C01-03) say how to judge level fit.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><em>Verbs:</em> the responsibilities use verbs such as own, define, partner and launch. Pick three that match work you have done and use the same words in your bullets, with your own evidence.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><em>Scope:</em> a posting that names one product area, a metric or a market tells you which of your stories to lead with.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><em>Mechanics:</em> note the documents, tests and any window. Dates and eligibility change, so check the posting yourself at the time you apply, and read the Dates box in this chapter.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A short example (<code>Illustrative</code>: the posting and the reader are invented). A posting asks for &quot;three years in product or analytics, SQL, and experience running experiments.&quot; The reader marks it: three years, partly (two in product and one in analytics, which still counts toward the total); SQL, yes (weekly funnel queries); experiments, partly (ran two tests, did not design them). The decision is to apply, lead with the funnel work, and describe the two tests honestly.</p>\n<p>Save the text of every posting you apply to, with the date you read it, because postings are edited or withdrawn. If you fail a requirement honestly, apply first to a posting you fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Where to go next.</strong> C01.5 for fit, the Dates box for what to check, C37.4 for projects that become stories, and C31 for how stories are probed. None of this replaces being ready for the loop; it only makes sure you get one.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c37-c37-3-weekly-cadence-and-practice-habits\"></span>\n<h4>C37.3 Weekly cadence and practice habits</h4>\n<p>A week of nine hours (adjust the block lengths, not the proportions) (FW-C37-03):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Block</th>\n<th>Hours</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Study</td>\n<td>2.0</td>\n<td>Read one part of the chapter on your path, then recompute its worked examples by hand, closed-book first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timed drills</td>\n<td>2.5</td>\n<td>Two or three exercises or questions aloud on the clock, from the week's target dimension (C34.6)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Partner mock</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>One scored mock with an outside scorer; record it; score independently before the debrief</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Project</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>Progress on the current story project (C37.4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Literacy</td>\n<td>1.0</td>\n<td>The weekly routine in C37.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Journal and review</td>\n<td>0.5</td>\n<td>Update the error log and answer journal; schedule the retest of one fix</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Habits that make it work.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Produce answers; do not reread.</strong> Retrieval practice beat rereading in the study behind CS-C34-02; feeling fluent after rereading is not evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Space the repeats.</strong> Redo a missed drill after a day, a week and a month rather than five times in one evening; spaced practice is widely reported to improve retention <code>[V: Cepeda et al., Psychological Bulletin, 2006]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>One fix at a time.</strong> Each week choose one error from the log and drill it (FW-C34-04).</li>\n<li><strong>Same time, same place.</strong> A fixed slot makes the habit cheap.</li>\n<li><strong>One rest day, and a rest week every quarter.</strong> Four of the 52 weeks are rest; plan them.</li>\n<li><strong>Protect sleep before a loop.</strong> C34.10 covers the final week.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Regional Lens: India.</strong> Loops with teams in other time zones are commonly held remotely, which can place interviews late in the evening or early in the morning in India <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: author's synthesis; no source checked]</code>. When you schedule a loop, ask for times in your time zone, plan sleep around them, run your mocks at similar hours at least twice, and test your connection and equipment in advance (C34.10). Also re-read the location and work-authorization lines of every posting (C01.4).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c37-c37-4-projects-that-become-interview-stories\"></span>\n<h4>C37.4 Projects that become interview stories</h4>\n<p>A story needs a decision, an action, a result and a number (C31); the only reliable way to have many is to do work that produces them. The projects below fit a product job or, if you lack access to product data, a public dataset or a side product. Every story must be true: record the real numbers, credit the people who helped, and do not describe a sandbox exercise as work you did in a company (FW-C37-04).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Project</th>\n<th>Months</th>\n<th>Deliverable</th>\n<th>Story it yields</th>\n<th>Numbers to capture</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>A metrics audit of your product: define five metrics with owners and guardrails; find one that misleads</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>A one-page metrics card per metric and a finding</td>\n<td>&quot;I found that our headline metric rose while retention was flat&quot; (C05, C29)</td>\n<td>The metric's definition, the gap, the decision it changed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>A small analysis or experiment: a funnel breakdown, a cohort comparison or a simple test</td>\n<td>4 to 5</td>\n<td>A readout with an interval and a decision</td>\n<td>&quot;I diagnosed a drop and recommended X&quot; (C13, C14)</td>\n<td>Sample, effect, interval, what shipped</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>A spec for a small improvement with non-goals and acceptance criteria, reviewed by an engineer</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>The spec and the review comments</td>\n<td>&quot;I wrote a spec that engineering built from&quot; (C16)</td>\n<td>Review changes, scope cut, delivery date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>An analytics sandbox: load a public dataset and answer three product questions in SQL</td>\n<td>2 to 4</td>\n<td>The queries, the outputs and a one-page conclusion</td>\n<td>Data fluency, not a work story (say so) (C37.6)</td>\n<td>Rows, the three results, one caveat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>A sourced teardown of a product with a tested change (a survey, a prototype test)</td>\n<td>5 to 7</td>\n<td>A labeled teardown and a test result</td>\n<td>&quot;I tested a change with five users&quot; (C28)</td>\n<td>Sources, test size, result</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>An influence project: get a decision made across functions</td>\n<td>8 to 9</td>\n<td>A decision document and the outcome</td>\n<td>&quot;I got a decision through without authority&quot; (C32)</td>\n<td>The stakeholders, the objection, the result</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Guidance.</strong> Start the first project in month 2 so that by month 6 you have at least four stories with numbers. If your job offers no way to do projects 1, 2, 3 and 6, ask your manager for a small scoped piece of one; a request to learn is easy to grant. Projects take about 1.5 hours a week; a story that needs more time is too big, so shrink it.</p>\n<span id=\"c37-c37-5-industry-and-company-literacy-habit-sources-and-weekly\"></span>\n<h4>C37.5 Industry and company literacy habit: sources and weekly cadence</h4>\n<p>Interviewers expect you to know the company's products and strategy at a level you can discuss with evidence. The habit is one hour a week, built on primary sources (FW-C37-05).</p>\n<p><strong>The weekly routine (60 minutes).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Scan (20 minutes):</em> the official newsrooms of two or three target companies for product launches and changes.</li>\n<li><em>Read one primary document (25 minutes):</em> a shareholder letter, a filing section, a launch post or a published study, noting three facts with the source and date.</li>\n<li><em>Apply (15 minutes):</em> write five lines on one launch: who it is for, what you would measure, one risk and one question you would ask the team (the &quot;what would you measure&quot; drill, C28).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Verified sources.</strong> The sources below were confirmed as existing official pages in the research for this book; confirm they are current before relying on them <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>What to use it for</th>\n<th>Status</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Google: The Keyword (blog.google), the company's central official blog</td>\n<td>Product announcements and leaders' posts</td>\n<td>Verified existing in research for this chapter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meta: Newsroom (about.fb.com/news)</td>\n<td>Product and policy announcements</td>\n<td>Verified existing in research for this chapter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Microsoft: The Official Microsoft Blog (blogs.microsoft.com)</td>\n<td>Company and product news</td>\n<td>Verified existing in research for this chapter (older post read; check the current layout)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apple: Newsroom (apple.com/newsroom)</td>\n<td>Product announcements</td>\n<td>Verified in research for C28</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Netflix: quarterly shareholder letters filed with the SEC, and the company's newsroom</td>\n<td>Strategy and metrics, in the company's own words</td>\n<td>Verified in research for C28</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Airbnb: newsroom (news.airbnb.com)</td>\n<td>Product releases and rationale</td>\n<td>Verified in research for C28</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Duolingo: annual report, registration statement and investor site</td>\n<td>A model of how a public product company reports metrics</td>\n<td>Verified in research for C28</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Amazon: Leadership Principles page on its careers site (amazon.jobs)</td>\n<td>The behavioral lens (C31)</td>\n<td>Read for C31</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SEC filings (EDGAR) for any public company</td>\n<td>Registration statements and annual reports</td>\n<td>Used for several cases in this book</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stripe: documentation and company pages</td>\n<td>Developer-product questions</td>\n<td>Not verified; check before use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Government service-delivery pages (for example a UK department's service-assessment page)</td>\n<td>Phase models for products (CS-C30-02)</td>\n<td>Verified existing in research for C30</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>What not to rely on.</strong> Preparation sites and forums are secondary and often outdated; use them to generate questions to check against a primary source, never as the source of a fact you will state (C28, FW-C28-02). Label anything you cannot source as an inference when you speak.</p>\n<p><strong>Turn it into company briefs.</strong> Each month, update the one-page brief for each target (C33.10): products, strategy signals with dates, three recent launches, a question for the team and the interview format as of that month. A brief with dated sources is evidence that you did the work.</p>\n<span id=\"c37-c37-6-practice-toolkit-and-knowledge-management-recording-no\"></span>\n<h4>C37.6 Practice toolkit and knowledge management: recording, note-taking, an analytics sandbox</h4>\n<p><strong>Recording.</strong> A phone voice or video recording, reviewed within 24 hours, with a timer visible; score it against the anchors before anyone else does (C34.1). Keep recordings from month 1 so that you can hear your own progress.</p>\n<p><strong>Note-taking: five files.</strong> (1) <code>story-bank</code>: one entry per episode with decision, action, result and number, and the probes you have prepared (C31.5). (2) <code>error-log</code>: the date, the round, the error, the fix and the retest date (FW-C34-04). (3) <code>journal</code>: the weekly answer journal. (4) <code>briefs</code>: one page per target company. (5) <code>sandbox</code>: the queries and outputs. Plain text or any notes tool is enough; what matters is that each file has one purpose, is dated and is searched before every mock.</p>\n<p><strong>Spaced review.</strong> Tag every missed drill with a retest date: a day, a week, a month later. A simple weekly list of &quot;due retests&quot; is the whole system.</p>\n<p><strong>An analytics sandbox.</strong> Data fluency is a skill you can practice alone. SQLite ships with Python's standard library, so a laptop is enough; free open-source engines such as DuckDB are an alternative <code>[V: Checked directly: import sqlite3 works in a stock Python install (standard library path); DuckDB project README: open-source, in-process, MIT license]</code>. The steps: load a public dataset (or generate one, as in Worked example 3), then answer three product questions: a funnel (how many users reach each step), a cohort retention (who returns), and a segment comparison (does a channel differ). For each, write the query, read the output, state one caveat (a small sample, survivorship, a definition problem) and one decision it would inform. Worked example 3 runs a complete example on synthetic data. The query patterns, the traps to avoid and the checks to run are in C13.6a (reading a query is in C21.4); C13.6a uses the same practice dataset and a second one for a mix shift (C13 Worked example 7).</p>\n<p><strong>Privacy and honesty.</strong> Do not paste confidential employer data, customer data or non-public company documents into practice tools, including AI practice partners (C34.9). Use synthetic or public data, and label sandbox work as such in an interview.</p>\n<span id=\"c37-c37-7-the-readiness-checklist-and-the-readiness-gate\"></span>\n<h4>C37.7 The readiness checklist and the readiness gate</h4>\n<p>A <strong>readiness gate</strong> is a written decision rule, set in advance, that says when you apply to your top targets: a list of measurable conditions that must all be true, with a re-test before each loop. It replaces two bad deciders: anxiety (&quot;not yet&quot;) and the calendar (&quot;it's time&quot;) (FW-C37-06). The thresholds are the author's heuristics built on the C34 scorecard and calibration, not any company's rubric <code>Original</code> <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Threshold to pass</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Outside-scored mocks (the last six)</td>\n<td>Average at least 4.0 on each high-weight competency (product sense, problem solving, analytics, execution, behavioral); at least 3.5 on the others; no single score of 2 or lower on a dimension central to a round in the last three mocks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Calibration</td>\n<td>Bias within ±0.5 and error at most 0.75 over at least six mocks (FW-C34-03)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Full loops</td>\n<td>Three full mock loops in the last six weeks, with an independent overall lean of &quot;lean hire&quot; or better in at least two</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Story bank</td>\n<td>Eight to ten stories with numbers; each survives two probes and a &quot;what would you do differently&quot; (C31)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Company briefs</td>\n<td>A dated brief for each target, with the interview format as of this month and a tailored &quot;why us&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Time and numbers</td>\n<td>Estimation and metric answers completed in time with a stated number in at least four of the last five drills</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Logistics and nerves</td>\n<td>The final-week protocol rehearsed once; a plan for a bad round (C34.8)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Fallback</td>\n<td>A written fallback plan (FW-C37-08)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>If one condition fails, hold the applications to top targets (wave 2) for a defined period, drill the specific gap, and re-test; keep applying to lower-stakes roles (wave 0 and 1) meanwhile.</p>\n<p><strong>The fallback plan (FW-C37-08).</strong> Decide in advance what you will do if the gate is not met by month 11 or if you have not received an offer by a stated date:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Diagnose from evidence:</em> compare actual interview feedback (where you were rejected, in which round type) with mock scores; look for a pattern.</li>\n<li><em>Widen the portfolio:</em> a loop is one draw from a selective process (C01.7); apply to more roles that fit (direct associate roles, internal transfers, smaller companies).</li>\n<li><em>Change one thing:</em> the weakest dimension, the round type where you fail, or the targets.</li>\n<li><em>Set the next date:</em> a re-test of the gate and a new application window; keep the habits and projects going.</li>\n<li><em>Protect yourself:</em> a rejection is data on one loop, not on you (C34.8).</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c37-c37-8-if-you-get-the-offer-your-first-90-days\"></span>\n<h4>C37.8 If you get the offer: your first 90 days</h4>\n<p>The same habits that got you the offer (a diagnostic baseline, outside feedback, small shipped work with numbers) are the first 90 days of the job (FW-C37-07). This is the concrete core of the &quot;landing and thriving&quot; material that Volume 2 will extend; it stays at the APM level and is <code>Inferred</code> from general descriptions of the role (C01.1) <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 30: learn the product and the system.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>The product:</em> use it as a user for several hours; read support tickets and the last two quarters of roadmap and launch reviews.</li>\n<li><em>The system:</em> map the team's decision rights (FW-C01-01), the partners (FW-C01-02) and the planning rhythm.</li>\n<li><em>The data:</em> find the team's metrics, how each is defined and what moved last quarter; run one query or read one dashboard yourself.</li>\n<li><em>The people:</em> a 30-minute one-to-one with each partner (engineering, design, data, support, legal) with the same questions: what do you need from a PM, what has gone well and badly, and what should I know that is not written down?</li>\n<li><em>Your manager:</em> agree what success looks like at 30, 60 and 90 days, in writing, and a weekly 30-minute check-in.</li>\n<li><em>Output:</em> a one-page summary of what you learned, with questions, shared with your manager by day 30.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Days 31 to 60: build relationships and contribute.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Own one small thing: a metric definition, a recurring report or a bug backlog review.</li>\n<li>Talk to users: at least ten conversations by day 60 (C06).</li>\n<li>Write one document that helps others: a decision document or a spec for a small change (C16, C32.2).</li>\n<li>Ask for feedback explicitly at day 45: one thing to keep, one to change.</li>\n<li>Earn credibility with engineering and design: ask for context, bring evidence, keep promises about dates and scope.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Days 61 to 90: ship one small thing well, and write down what you learned.</strong></p>\n";
